{"repositoryId":363909232,"availability":"available","full":"Axual/ksml","name":"ksml","description":"Kafka Streams without Java","category":"Developer tools","language":"Java","stars":37,"forks":14,"created":"2021-05-03T11:37:20Z","updated":"2026-10-16T12:26:54Z","license":"Apache-2.0","branch":"main","homepage":"https://axual.github.io/ksml/","topics":["apachekafka","kafka-streams","kafkastreams","lowcode","stream-processing","streamprocessing"],"demo":"https://axual.github.io/ksml/","color":"#193546","ink":"#a6d6e8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-17T07:12:59.151Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-17T07:12:59.151Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-17T07:12:59.151Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-17T07:12:59.151Z","stars":37,"forks":14}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["KSML is a wrapper language and interpreter around Kafka Streams that lets you express any topology in a YAML syntax. Simply define your topology as a processing pipeline with a series of steps that your data passes through. Your custom functions can be expressed inline in Python. KSML will read your definition and construct the topology dynamically via the Kafka Streams DSL and run it in GraalVM.","KSML was started by Axual in early 2021 and open-sourced in May 2021.","Kafka Streams is powerful but Java-centric. KSML eliminates the Java boiler-plate through:"],"features":["Declarative YAML for topology wiring","User-defined functions in Python for customized business logic","One command to package and run (container image or in your own JVM)"],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/Axual/ksml/blob/main/README.md","fingerprint":"323ec8bb16bc81b602512d07ce867a94a117fcfe783b0ba2652782b61366d5e2","extractedAt":"2026-10-17T07:12:59.276Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-17T07:12:59.276Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"KSML is a wrapper language and interpreter around Kafka Streams that lets you express any topology in a YAML syntax. Simply define your topology as a processing p